/* Original EECS 427/627 lecture figures with verified captions (generated). */
window.TAPEOUT_LECTURES = {"427-2": "Fabrication and Layout", "427-3": "Static CMOS Circuits Review: Delay", "427-4": "Logical Effort", "427-5": "Adders", "427-6": "Static Logic Families", "427-7": "Dynamic Logic Families", "427-8": "Dynamic Power", "427-9": "Static Power (Leakage)", "427-10": "SRAM", "427-11": "Timing and Latch Design", "427-12": "Multipliers", "427-13": "Interconnect", "627-1": "Digital Noise", "627-2": "Synchronization", "627-3": "Low Power", "627-4": "Power Supply", "627-5": "Silicon on Insulator (SOI)", "627-6": "Energy Recovery (Adiabatic Logic)", "627-7": "Variations", "627-8": "Adaptive Design", "627-9": "Leakage", "627-10": "Inductance", "627-11": "Compute-in-Memory"};
window.TAPEOUT_SLIDES = [{"lec":"427-2","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p6-1.png","cap":"The MOS gate stack is a capacitor, and the gate voltage relative to the body controls whether a channel forms between n+ source and drain.","pg":6,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-2","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p6-2.png","cap":"Static CMOS pairs a pMOS pull-up network with a complementary nMOS pull-down network, so exactly one of them conducts in steady state.","pg":6,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-2","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p7-1.png","cap":"In a NAND2 the internal node Z between the two series nMOS takes different values depending on the input pattern, including a degraded VDD−Vth.","pg":7,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-2","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p7-2.png","cap":"In a NOR2 the internal node between the series pMOS can sit at a degraded Vth or float near VDD.","pg":7,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-2","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p8-1.png","cap":"A tristate buffer drives 0 or 1 when enabled and presents high impedance Z when not enabled.","pg":8,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-2","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p8-2.png","cap":"A tristate inverter puts EN-controlled transistors in series with an inverter stack, so its output is restored to the rails.","pg":8,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-2","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p8-3.png","cap":"On a p-substrate the nMOS sits directly in the substrate while the pMOS needs an n-well as its body.","pg":8,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-2","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p8-4.png","cap":"Metal on lightly doped silicon makes a poor contact, so heavily doped n+ well taps and p+ substrate taps tie the bodies to VDD and GND.","pg":8,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-2","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p9-1.png","cap":"The inverter is defined by a set of masks, and the cross-section is taken along the dashed line through both transistors and both taps.","pg":9,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-2","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p9-2.png","cap":"Six masks define the inverter: n-well, polysilicon, n+ diffusion, p+ diffusion, contact and metal.","pg":9,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-2","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p9-3.png","cap":"Photolithography transfers the mask pattern into photoresist, and the resist then protects the oxide during etch.","pg":9,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-2","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p9-4.png","cap":"N-diffusion is self-aligned: the already-patterned poly gate blocks the implant, so source and drain line up exactly with the gate.","pg":9,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-2","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p10-1.png","cap":"Contacts connect M1 to diffusion and poly, and vias connect each metal layer up through the stack to top metal.","pg":10,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-2","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p10-2.png","cap":"Minimum mask dimensions set transistor size, and the feature size is the minimum poly width, which sets L_gate.","pg":10,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-2","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p11-1.png","cap":"Design rules exist to tolerate fabrication errors such as mask misalignment, dust, lateral diffusion and rough surfaces.","pg":11,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-2","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p11-2.png","cap":"A transistor is the overlap of poly and diffusion, so poly must extend past diffusion, and diffusion should never be used for routing.","pg":11,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-2","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p11-3.png","cap":"The inverter layout annotates the key rule types: poly extension, contact to diffusion, n-well enclosure of p-diffusion, metal spacing and poly-to-contact spacing.","pg":11,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-2","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p11-4.png","cap":"Contact and via rules require both layers to enclose the cut so that a misaligned mask still lands the contact on both materials.","pg":11,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-2","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p12-1.png","cap":"Plasma processing charges exposed conductors, and if a large conductor area connects only to a thin gate the gate oxide can break down.","pg":12,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-2","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p12-2.png","cap":"Antenna violations are fixed by jumping to a higher metal near the gate or by adding a reverse-biased diode to discharge the line.","pg":12,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-2","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p13-1.png","cap":"The parasitic vertical PNP and lateral NPN form a thyristor that, once triggered through Rwell and Rsub, latches a low-impedance VDD-to-GND path.","pg":13,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-2","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p13-2.png","cap":"Latch-up is avoided by lowering well and substrate resistance with heavy doping, close taps and guard rings.","pg":13,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-2","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p14-1.png","cap":"In 130 nm the minimum device is 160 nm wide by 120 nm long, the reference inverter is 320/120 pMOS over 160/120 nMOS, and NAND2/NOR2 share diffusion between adjacent transistors.","pg":14,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-2","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p15-1.png","cap":"A simplified layout diagram shows only metal1, p-diffusion, n-diffusion, poly and contacts for the inverter and a three-input gate.","pg":15,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-2","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p15-2.png","cap":"A stick diagram has no dimensions and shows only the relative positions of transistors, wires and contacts.","pg":15,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-2","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p16-1.png","cap":"Yield is good dies divided by total dies, dies per wafer falls with die area including an edge-loss term, and yield falls exponentially with area times defect density.","pg":16,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-3","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p18-1.png","cap":"Propagation delay is measured from the 50% point of the input to the 50% point of the output, and rise and fall times from 10% to 90%.","pg":18,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-3","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p18-2.png","cap":"A step into an RC network gives an exponential output that crosses 50% at t = ln2·RC ≈ 0.69RC.","pg":18,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-3","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p19-1.png","cap":"For a low-to-high output the pMOS charges CL through RP, and for high-to-low the nMOS discharges CL through RN.","pg":19,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-3","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p19-2.png","cap":"The equivalent resistance averages V/I at the start and midpoint of the transition and is about three quarters of VDD over IDSAT.","pg":19,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-3","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p20-1.png","cap":"Gate capacitance is modeled simply as Cox·W·L, which is about 1.3 fF per micron of gate width in this process.","pg":20,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-3","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p20-2.png","cap":"Source and drain diffusion capacitance is parasitic, depends on area and perimeter, and is about CG for a contacted diffusion and half that for an uncontacted one.","pg":20,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-3","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p20-3.png","cap":"Good layout shares contacted diffusions and merges uncontacted ones, which in the NAND3 example cuts output capacitance by 2C.","pg":20,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-3","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p20-4.png","cap":"In 130 nm, C is about 1.3 fF per micron so 0.21 fF per unit device, and R is about 13 kΩ for a 160 nm wide unit nMOS.","pg":20,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-3","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p21-1.png","cap":"A fanout-of-1 inverter with 10 kΩ drive and 1.26 fF total load has a delay of about 8.7 ps.","pg":21,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-3","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p21-2.png","cap":"Delay falls with VDD roughly as CL·VDD over (VDD−VT) to the alpha, with alpha about 1.2 to 1.3.","pg":21,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-3","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p22-1.png","cap":"For a fixed load, upsizing the driver by S reduces delay toward an asymptote set by its own intrinsic capacitance.","pg":22,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-3","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p22-2.png","cap":"Raising the P/N ratio beta speeds up the rising transition but slows the falling one, so average tp has a minimum, which is not at the tr = tf point.","pg":22,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-3","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p23-1.png","cap":"NAND2 rising delay is 0.69 Rp/2 CL if both inputs fall but 0.69 Rp CL if only one falls, and falling delay is 0.69 2Rn CL.","pg":23,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-3","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p23-2.png","cap":"Placing the late-arriving input on the transistor closest to the output means the internal node is already discharged when it switches.","pg":23,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-3","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p24-1.png","cap":"Size each network so its worst-case path has the resistance of the reference inverter: NAND2 nMOS 2 and pMOS 2, NOR2 pMOS 4 and nMOS 1.","pg":24,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-3","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p24-2.png","cap":"For OUT = NOT(D + A(B+C)) the series paths set the widths, and there are multiple valid pMOS sizings such as 4-8-8-4 or 3-6-6-6.","pg":24,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-3","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p25-1.png","cap":"Elmore delay sums, for each node, its capacitance times the total resistance from that node back to the source.","pg":25,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-3","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p25-2.png","cap":"A unit NAND3 driving f identical gates has worst-case falling delay (10.5 + 5f)RC and pull-up delay (9 + 5f)RC by Elmore.","pg":25,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-3","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p26-1.png","cap":"Two NAND2 stick layouts differ in whether the output sits on a shared middle pMOS diffusion or on two outer diffusions, which changes output capacitance.","pg":26,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-3","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p26-2.png","cap":"Folding a wide transistor into two fingers shares the drain between them and reduces diffusion capacitance.","pg":26,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-4","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p28-1.png","cap":"Gate delay in units of tau splits into effort delay f = gh and parasitic delay p.","pg":28,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-4","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p28-2.png","cap":"On a delay-versus-fanout plot, g is the slope and p is the intercept, with the inverter as the reference g = p = 1.","pg":28,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-4","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p29-1.png","cap":"Logical effort is the input capacitance of a gate divided by that of an inverter delivering the same output current, so inverter 3/3, NAND2 4/3, NOR2 5/3.","pg":29,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-4","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p29-2.png","cap":"For a 2:1 reference, an n-input NAND has g = (n+2)/3 and an n-input NOR has g = (2n+1)/3.","pg":29,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-4","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p30-1.png","cap":"Parasitic delay is estimated from output self-loading at equal drive: inverter 3/3 = 1, NAND2 6/3 = 2, NOR2 6/3 = 2.","pg":30,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-4","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p30-2.png","cap":"Normalized delay versus electrical effort shows lines whose slope is g and intercept is p, for example a NAND3 at d = (5/3)h + 3.","pg":30,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-4","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p31-1.png","cap":"An inverter driving four copies of itself has h = 4, g = 1, p = 1, so the FO4 delay is 5 tau, roughly L/3 ps.","pg":31,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-4","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p31-2.png","cap":"A gate with Cin = 1 driving three loads totaling 3 has h = 3, and as a NAND4 with g = 6/3 and p = 4 its delay is 10 tau.","pg":31,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-4","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p31-3.png","cap":"With branching, part of the current goes off-path, so the product of stage efforts exceeds H and must include the branching effort b.","pg":31,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-4","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p31-4.png","cap":"Path effort F = GBH is the product of stage efforts and depends only on topology, not on sizes or added inverters.","pg":31,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-4","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p32-1.png","cap":"Setting the derivative of total delay with respect to h1 to zero gives g1h1 = g2h2, so delay is minimal when stage efforts are equal.","pg":32,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-4","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p32-2.png","cap":"For N stages the optimum stage effort is F to the 1/N, and the minimum path delay is N times F to the 1/N plus P.","pg":32,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-4","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p33-1.png","cap":"Three NAND2 stages from Cin = 4 to CL = 108 have F = 64, an optimal stage effort of 4 and a minimum delay of 18 tau.","pg":33,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-4","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p33-2.png","cap":"Working backward from the load with Cin = g·Cout/f gives input caps 36, 12 and 4, and the last NAND2 uses transistors of width 18.","pg":33,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-4","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p34-1.png","cap":"Adding n2 inverters leaves F unchanged but adds parasitic delay, so the optimum number of stages is a balance that depends on technology.","pg":34,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-4","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p34-2.png","cap":"With p_inv = 1 the best number of stages rises by one roughly every factor of 4 in path effort, and the best stage effort stays between about 2.4 and 4.4.","pg":34,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-4","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p35-1.png","cap":"A 4-to-16 decoder for a 16-word by 32-bit register file drives 96 units of wordline capacitance from address inputs of 10 units each.","pg":35,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-4","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p35-2.png","cap":"With H = 9.6, B = 8, G = 2, F = 153.6 the best design is 3 stages with stage effort 5.35, delay 22.1 tau, and sizes 10, 6.7 and 18.","pg":35,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-4","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p35-3.png","cap":"A spreadsheet comparison of decoder topologies shows NAND2-INV-NAND2-INV is fastest at 19.7 tau and a single NOR4 is terrible at 234 tau.","pg":35,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-4","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p35-4.png","cap":"Using more stages than optimal is less harmful than using fewer: delay is 1.51 times optimal at half the stages but only 1.26 times at double.","pg":35,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-4","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p36-1.png","cap":"Sizing a single gate wrong by 1.5x or 0.67x costs only about 4 percent of delay, and even 2x or 0.5x costs about 13 percent.","pg":36,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-4","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p36-2.png","cap":"P/N = 2 balances noise margins and slopes, but P/N = 1.5 lowers input capacitance and can give a faster average delay.","pg":36,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-4","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p37-1.png","cap":"Internal-node capacitance and body effect make the bottom and middle inputs of a stack slower, which the simple logical-effort model ignores.","pg":37,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-4","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p37-2.png","cap":"Measured in the class's 130 nm process, tau is about 6.3 ps and p_inv about 3.4 at nominal, with clear spread across fast and slow corners.","pg":37,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-5","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p39-1.png","cap":"A full adder is S = A⊕B⊕C and Cout = MAJ(A,B,C); the truth table splits into kill, propagate and generate rows.","pg":39,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-5","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p39-2.png","cap":"Generate, propagate and kill describe what a bit does to the incoming carry, using only A and B.","pg":39,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-5","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p40-1.png","cap":"Carry and sum can be written as Co = G + P·Ci and S = P⊕Ci, and P = A+B works for the carry but not the sum.","pg":40,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-5","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p41-1.png","cap":"The 28-transistor static adder computes ¬Cout first and then reuses it to build the sum.","pg":41,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-5","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p41-2.png","cap":"Inverting all inputs inverts both outputs, so a ripple chain can alternate true and complemented bits and drop inverters.","pg":41,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-5","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p42-1.png","cap":"The 24-transistor mirror adder uses symmetric NMOS and PMOS networks, with Ci transistors placed closest to the output.","pg":42,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-5","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p42-2.png","cap":"Layout rules for the mirror adder focus on minimizing capacitance at the carry node Co.","pg":42,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-5","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p43-1.png","cap":"Ripple carry delay grows linearly with the number of bits, so the goal is the fastest possible carry cell.","pg":43,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-5","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p43-2.png","cap":"Using P and G, the ripple adder splits into a setup stage, a carry chain of AND-OR cells, and a sum XOR.","pg":43,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-5","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p43-3.png","cap":"Carry-bypass skips a block when all its bits propagate, selecting Cin directly via a mux.","pg":43,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-5","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p43-4.png","cap":"The bypass critical path ripples through the first block, bypasses the middle blocks, and ripples through the last block.","pg":43,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-5","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p44-1.png","cap":"The carry-select adder precomputes both possible block carries and a multiplexer picks the right one when the real carry arrives.","pg":44,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-5","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p45-1.png","cap":"In a linear carry-select adder the critical path is one block ripple plus one mux per block.","pg":45,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-5","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p45-2.png","cap":"Square-root carry select grows block sizes by one so each block's carries are ready just as the incoming carry arrives.","pg":45,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-5","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p46-1.png","cap":"Ripple is linear, linear-select grows in steps, and square-root select grows slowest with word length.","pg":46,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-5","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p47-1.png","cap":"Black cells combine group generate and propagate, gray cells compute only generate, and buffers just pass signals.","pg":47,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-5","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p47-2.png","cap":"The ripple adder drawn as a PG diagram is a serial chain of N−1 gray cells.","pg":47,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-5","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p47-3.png","cap":"Kogge-Stone reaches every group G in log2N levels with fanout of 2, at the cost of many long wires.","pg":47,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-5","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p47-4.png","cap":"Brent-Kung uses a forward tree and a backward tree, giving low fanout and few wires but 2log2(n)−1 levels.","pg":47,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-5","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p48-1.png","cap":"Sklansky reaches log2(n) levels with few wires but fanout that doubles each level and uneven sizing.","pg":48,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-5","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p48-2.png","cap":"Every prefix tree trades logic levels, fanout and wiring, and the three corners are Kogge-Stone, Brent-Kung and Sklansky.","pg":48,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-5","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p48-3.png","cap":"Han-Carlson does Kogge-Stone on every other bit and one ripple stage at the end, halving wiring.","pg":48,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-5","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p48-4.png","cap":"Knowles halves Kogge-Stone wiring by letting neighbors share overlapping groups, at the cost of more fanout.","pg":48,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-5","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p49-1.png","cap":"Ladner-Fischer mixes Brent-Kung, Sklansky and Han-Carlson layers to trade logic levels against fanout with low wiring.","pg":49,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-5","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p49-2.png","cap":"A higher-radix cell merges four groups at once, G = G3 + P3(G2 + P2(G1 + P1G0)).","pg":49,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-5","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p49-3.png","cap":"Sparse trees compute only every s-th carry and use carry-select blocks to finish the sums.","pg":49,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-6","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p51-1.png","cap":"Static CMOS is ratioless, full-swing and nearly zero static power, which is why it is the default.","pg":51,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-6","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p52-1.png","cap":"A pulse-static path is reset to a known state by a clock and then evaluates with only one transition per gate.","pg":52,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-6","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p52-2.png","cap":"Because each gate in evaluation only switches one way, its P/N ratio can be skewed to speed that edge.","pg":52,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-6","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p53-1.png","cap":"PS-CMOS is fast through skewing, has no delay dependence on history, and does not glitch in evaluation.","pg":53,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-6","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p53-2.png","cap":"PS-CMOS cannot make arbitrary connections, burns more power, and has worse noise margin from skewed ratios.","pg":53,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-6","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p53-3.png","cap":"DCVS uses complementary NMOS pull-down trees with a cross-coupled PMOS pair as the load.","pg":53,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-6","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p53-4.png","cap":"In DCVS the side pulled down by the NMOS tree moves first, then the cross-coupled PMOS pulls the other side up.","pg":53,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-6","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p54-1.png","cap":"DCVS needs no PMOS duality, so input capacitance is lower and complex functions fit in one stage.","pg":54,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-6","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p54-2.png","cap":"DCVS needs dual-rail inputs, has crowbar current, and the PMOS must be sized in a narrow window.","pg":54,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-6","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p55-1.png","cap":"CNTL adds clamp transistors so internal nodes swing only between about Vt and Vdd−Vt.","pg":55,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-6","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p55-2.png","cap":"CNTL rises faster and draws a smaller current spike because the PMOS is never hard on or off.","pg":55,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-6","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p55-3.png","cap":"CNTL pays with higher leakage, more devices, careful sizing, and a non-full-swing output that must be restored.","pg":55,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-6","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p56-1.png","cap":"Pass-gate logic is 20-50% faster in XOR- and mux-like circuits because it carries less capacitance.","pg":56,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-6","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p56-2.png","cap":"Pass gates lose a threshold, suffer body effect, and are vulnerable to input undershoot and coupling.","pg":56,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-6","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p57-1.png","cap":"Pass-gate delay depends on state, and the output needs an inverter before it can drive the next stage.","pg":57,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-6","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p57-2.png","cap":"LEAP adds an output inverter with a PMOS feedback keeper to restore full swing.","pg":57,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-6","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p58-1.png","cap":"With a keeper the internal node reaches full rail, but the input sees more loading and current during switching.","pg":58,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-6","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p59-1.png","cap":"Transmission gates pair NMOS and PMOS so either level passes cleanly, with better noise margin but more capacitance.","pg":59,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-6","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p59-2.png","cap":"Simulated side by side, the transmission gate reaches the full rail while the NMOS pass gate stops short.","pg":59,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-6","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p60-1.png","cap":"CPL builds dual-rail NMOS pass networks with a cross-coupled PMOS restorer and output inverters.","pg":60,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-6","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p60-2.png","cap":"Unlike DCVS, CPL inputs drive both trees and PMOS sizing affects only speed and noise margin.","pg":60,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-6","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p60-3.png","cap":"CPL is fast but has poor noise margin, needs buffered inputs, and its PMOS size trades noise and power against speed.","pg":60,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-6","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p61-1.png","cap":"OPL predicts every output is 1, so in an inverting path half the gates never need to switch.","pg":61,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-6","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p61-2.png","cap":"Precharging toward the middle and evaluating early cuts delay versus a classic dynamic gate.","pg":61,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-6","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p61-3.png","cap":"Clock timing relative to inputs decides whether the OPL output glitches down and recovers or stays high.","pg":61,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-6","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p61-4.png","cap":"OPL gates swing about half rail and are over 2x faster than CMOS, but clocking, variation and power make them hard to use.","pg":61,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-7","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p63-1.png","cap":"A dynamic gate precharges its node high when the clock is low and conditionally discharges it through the NMOS network when the clock is high.","pg":63,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-7","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p63-2.png","cap":"The dynamic 3-input OR has far less input and internal capacitance than the static version.","pg":63,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-7","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p64-1.png","cap":"Domino is faster with lower input capacitance and an early switch point, using a HI-skew output inverter.","pg":64,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-7","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p64-2.png","cap":"Domino pays with low noise margin, charge sharing, leakage, and a noise-sensitive floating node.","pg":64,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-7","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p65-1.png","cap":"During evaluate a non-discharged dynamic node floats, and NMOS leakage slowly discharges it.","pg":65,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-7","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p65-2.png","cap":"The keeper current must sit between the total off-leakage of the pull-down and the on-current of a single pull-down path.","pg":65,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-7","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p66-1.png","cap":"A static PMOS pull-up network as keeper avoids the fight, but only a few PMOS fit in series and input cap rises.","pg":66,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-7","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p66-2.png","cap":"In evaluate the dynamic node shares charge with an internal node that was discharged in a previous cycle.","pg":66,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-7","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p66-3.png","cap":"Precharging internal nodes fixes charge sharing but adds capacitance and makes the pull-down slower.","pg":66,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-7","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p67-1.png","cap":"Domino delay depends on input arrival time, and the first gate in a chain sees extra clock-related delay.","pg":67,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-7","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p68-1.png","cap":"A domino chain precharges every gate together and then evaluates in a ripple, with inputs held stable by latches during evaluate.","pg":68,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-7","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p69-1.png","cap":"Only the first stage needs a footer, provided later stages see their inputs precharge low before their own precharge.","pg":69,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-7","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p69-2.png","cap":"Delayed clocks let each stage precharge after its inputs have fallen and evaluate before its inputs become valid.","pg":69,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-7","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p69-3.png","cap":"Footless domino needs multiple clocks and gives later stages less precharge time, traded against one less series NMOS.","pg":69,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-7","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p70-1.png","cap":"NORA alternates N-type gates precharged high with P-type gates precharged low, with no inverter in between.","pg":70,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-7","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p70-2.png","cap":"NORA is fast but drives loads poorly, uses slow PMOS networks, and has poor noise margin.","pg":70,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-7","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p70-3.png","cap":"Dual-rail domino computes both f and its complement so any logic function is possible, at twice the transistors.","pg":70,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-7","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p70-4.png","cap":"When evaluation trees share internal nodes, those nodes are precharged for every input pattern.","pg":70,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-7","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p71-1.png","cap":"Cross-coupled keepers in dual-rail domino avoid a keeper fight but leave both rails unprotected until inputs arrive.","pg":71,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-7","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p71-2.png","cap":"Multiple-output domino taps intermediate nodes of one pull-down stack to compute several functions at once.","pg":71,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-7","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p72-1.png","cap":"Compound domino ends short dynamic stacks in a static gate, e.g. a NAND that computes AB + CD.","pg":72,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-7","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p72-2.png","cap":"Self-resetting domino has no clock or footer; its own output, delayed through an inverter chain, triggers the precharge.","pg":72,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-7","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p72-3.png","cap":"SRD requires inputs to fall before the reset pulse and the reset to finish before the next input arrives.","pg":72,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-7","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p73-1.png","cap":"SRD trades the clock for hard pulse-timing constraints that are sensitive to process variation.","pg":73,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-7","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p74-1.png","cap":"LSDL puts a clocked latch behind a dynamic front end so the output changes only when its value changes.","pg":74,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-7","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p74-2.png","cap":"In LSDL the dynamic node precharges every cycle, but the latched output switches only on a real change.","pg":74,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-7","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p74-3.png","cap":"LSDL gives up keepers and dynamic depth per stage, needs narrow evaluate pulses, and complicates setup and hold analysis.","pg":74,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-7","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p75-1.png","cap":"When a static gate driven by a dynamic node turns on, its rising gate capacitance pulls charge off the dynamic node.","pg":75,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-7","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p75-2.png","cap":"Clock feedthrough through the precharge device's gate-drain capacitance bumps the dynamic node at clock edges.","pg":75,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-8","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p77-1.png","cap":"Total power splits into dynamic switching, short-circuit (crowbar) and static leakage components.","pg":77,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-8","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p77-2.png","cap":"Short-circuit current is a brief overlap blip, under 10% of dynamic power when input and output slopes are comparable.","pg":77,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-8","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p78-1.png","cap":"On a rising output the supply delivers C·VDD², half burned in the pMOS and half stored on the load.","pg":78,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-8","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p78-2.png","cap":"On the falling output the supply delivers nothing and the stored ½C·VDD² is dissipated in the nMOS.","pg":78,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-8","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p79-1.png","cap":"Dynamic power is α·C·VDD²·f, with α = 1 for a clock and ½ for a node that toggles once per cycle.","pg":79,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-8","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p79-2.png","cap":"For random independent data α = P·(1−P), giving 0.25 at P = 0.5 and typically about 0.1 in real logic.","pg":79,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-8","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p80-1.png","cap":"Output-high probability for each gate follows from the input probabilities, assuming independent inputs.","pg":80,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-8","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p80-2.png","cap":"A 4-input AND built as NAND2-NAND2 into an inverted-input AND (a NOR2) has α = 3/16 on the internal nodes and 15/256 on the output.","pg":80,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-8","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p81-1.png","cap":"A 1-billion-transistor 65 nm chip at 1 V and 1 GHz, with logic at α = 0.1 and memory at α = 0.02.","pg":81,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-8","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p81-2.png","cap":"Switched capacitance is 27 nF of logic and 171 nF of memory, giving about 6.1 W of dynamic power.","pg":81,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-8","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p82-1.png","cap":"Under ideal (Dennard) scaling, power per device falls as 1/S² and power density stays constant.","pg":82,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-8","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p82-2.png","cap":"Power rose anyway because device count grew, VDD stopped scaling, leakage grew and interconnect increased.","pg":82,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-8","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p83-1.png","cap":"A transmission-gate latch with a local clock inverter puts 8C on clock, while a pass-nMOS latch moves the load to data (14C data, 2C clock).","pg":83,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-8","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p83-2.png","cap":"Clock gating stops the clock to idle registers, removing α = 1 clock switching and all downstream activity.","pg":83,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-8","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p84-1.png","cap":"The reference datapath (adder + comparator) runs at 40 MHz at 5 V with switched capacitance Cref.","pg":84,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-8","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p84-2.png","cap":"Two parallel copies at half clock rate allow VDD = Vref/1.7, giving 0.36 Pref at 2.15× capacitance.","pg":84,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-8","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p84-3.png","cap":"Pipelining halves the critical path so VDD = Vref/1.7 at the same clock, giving 0.39 Pref with only 1.15× capacitance.","pg":84,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-8","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p84-4.png","cap":"Summary: pipelined 0.39, parallel 0.36, pipeline plus parallel 0.2 of reference power at 2.0 V and 3.7× area.","pg":84,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-8","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p85-1.png","cap":"Two supplies are common; a low-VDD gate driving a high-VDD gate needs a level converter because the pMOS never fully turns off.","pg":85,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-8","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p85-2.png","cap":"The DCVS level converter uses cross-coupled pMOS and differential nMOS pull-downs, at the cost of contention and more transistors.","pg":85,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-8","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p86-1.png","cap":"The pass-gate level converter lets a VDDL-gated pass nMOS pull the input node low, with a single pMOS feedback keeper, reducing contention.","pg":86,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-8","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p86-2.png","cap":"A diode-connected Vt-drop transistor avoids a second supply, giving swing Vt to VDD−Vt and power α·C·VDD·(VDD−2Vt)·f.","pg":86,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-8","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p87-1.png","cap":"Intel's dual-supply latch accepts a low-VDD clock on nMOS-only clock devices; the test chip cut clock power by 71%.","pg":87,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-8","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p88-1.png","cap":"Clock/power gating saves energy linearly with duty cycle, while just-in-time DVFS saves roughly with the cube.","pg":88,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-9","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p90-1.png","cap":"I1 is reverse-biased junction leakage, typically below 1 fA/µm² and negligible, plus band-to-band tunneling.","pg":90,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-9","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p90-2.png","cap":"I2 is subthreshold (weak-inversion) conduction, the dominant leakage in modern devices.","pg":90,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-9","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p91-1.png","cap":"Subthreshold current is exponential in Vgs; rewriting it relative to Ioff gives a base-10 form with slope S ≈ 100 mV/decade.","pg":91,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-9","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p91-2.png","cap":"DIBL lowers the threshold by η·Vds, so higher drain voltage increases leakage.","pg":91,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-9","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p92-1.png","cap":"Reverse body bias raises Vt and cuts leakage; leakage is flat in Vds above 4vT (except DIBL) and drops sharply below 2vT.","pg":92,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-9","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p92-2.png","cap":"I3 is gate-induced drain leakage: with negative gate and positive drain, tunneling at the drain edge under the gate.","pg":92,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-9","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p92-3.png","cap":"On log Ids vs Vgs: S = 100 mV/decade, DIBL moves Ioff from 2.0 to 27 nA/µm between Vds = 0.1 and 1.0 V, and GIDL lifts the curve at negative Vgs.","pg":92,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-9","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p92-4.png","cap":"For Vds above 50 mV, Isub ≈ Ioff·10^((Vgs + η(Vds−VDD) − kγVsb)/S), with typical 65 nm values Ioff = 1-100 nA/µm and η = kγ = 0.1.","pg":92,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-9","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p93-1.png","cap":"I4 is gate-oxide tunneling, rising sharply with thinner oxide and depending strongly on oxide material.","pg":93,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-9","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p93-2.png","cap":"Gate leakage is exponentially sensitive to tox and VDD, larger for electrons (nMOS), negligible above 30 Å and critical near 12 Å until high-k.","pg":93,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-9","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p94-1.png","cap":"The three levers: +100 mV of Vt cuts leakage ~10× but slows the gate, cooling cuts it ~5.2× per 10 °C, and stacking cuts it further.","pg":94,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-9","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p94-2.png","cap":"Two series OFF devices self-reverse-bias: Vx = ηVDD/(1 + 2η + kγ) and leakage drops about 10×.","pg":94,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-9","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p95-1.png","cap":"Simulated stack leakage: SVT 258 → 36.1 → 19.8 pA for 1/2/3-stacks; HVT 1.25 → 0.185 → 0.122 pA.","pg":95,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-9","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p95-2.png","cap":"In a NAND3, leakage ranges from 10.5 pA (all inputs 0) to 192 pA (all 1); dominant states have only one OFF device in the path.","pg":95,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-9","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p96-1.png","cap":"Single-gate leakage varies up to 101× across states, but whole blocks vary only 1.2-1.8× because many gates average out.","pg":96,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-9","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p96-2.png","cap":"Find the minimum-leakage input vector (30-40% variation) and force it with sleep-controlled latches.","pg":96,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-9","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p97-1.png","cap":"Power gating uses high-Vt header/footer switches to create virtual rails, with output isolation; the switch costs IR drop when on and dynamic power to toggle.","pg":97,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-9","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p97-2.png","cap":"A state-retaining MTCMOS latch keeps a high-Vt, always-powered storage loop so data survives standby.","pg":97,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-9","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p98-1.png","cap":"Use low Vt only on critical paths; assignment per gate or per transistor needs no clustering and no level converters.","pg":98,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-9","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p98-2.png","cap":"Vt can be assigned per gate, per pull-up/pull-down half, per stack, or per transistor, with finer control costing library size and design-rule area.","pg":98,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-9","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p99-1.png","cap":"Dynamic body bias applies about 450 mV forward bias in active mode for speed and about 500 mV reverse bias in idle mode for leakage, needing a triple well.","pg":99,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-9","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p99-2.png","cap":"VTCMOS biases the n-well and an isolated p-well separately (VBBP, VBBN) to move Vt at run time.","pg":99,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-9","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p100-1.png","cap":"Four techniques: MTCMOS (high-Vt switches), dual-Vt, state assignment, and variable-Vt via substrate bias.","pg":100,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-9","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p100-2.png","cap":"Dynamic and leakage power knobs sorted by design time, run time, and adaptive (variable throughput).","pg":100,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-10","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p103-1.png","cap":"An SRAM is a 2^n-row by 2^m-column array of cells with a row decoder, bitline conditioning on top and column circuitry at the bottom.","pg":103,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-10","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p103-2.png","cap":"The 6T cell stores a bit in cross-coupled inverters and reaches the bitlines through two access transistors gated by the wordline.","pg":103,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-10","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p104-1.png","cap":"The SNM is the side of the largest square that fits inside the butterfly curve's lobes, here 0.32 V.","pg":104,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-10","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p105-1.png","cap":"During a read the bitline on the 0 side discharges through access and pull-down devices, and the 0 node bumps up.","pg":105,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-10","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p105-2.png","cap":"The access transistor and pull-down NMOS form a divider that raises node A while bit discharges.","pg":105,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-10","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p106-1.png","cap":"The read bump on node A falls as the cell ratio rises, so a CR around 1.2 to 2 keeps the bump well below the trip point.","pg":106,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-10","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p106-2.png","cap":"Sizing order: pull-down strong, access medium, pull-up weak.","pg":106,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-10","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p106-3.png","cap":"To write, drive one bitline low and raise the wordline; the access device must overpower the PMOS pull-up so A_b falls and the cell flips.","pg":106,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-10","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p106-4.png","cap":"The A_b voltage during write rises with the pull-up ratio, so the PMOS must be weak relative to the access NMOS.","pg":106,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-10","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p107-1.png","cap":"A textbook 6T cell is 26 by 45 lambda; tiles mirror to share VDD, GND and bitline contacts.","pg":107,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-10","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p107-2.png","cap":"Thin-cell layout keeps poly and diffusion straight and in one direction, and also shortens the bitlines.","pg":107,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-10","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p108-1.png","cap":"Five Intel generations show steady cell-area scaling, with the switch to the thin cell at 65 nm.","pg":108,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-10","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p108-2.png","cap":"A published 65 nm cell uses pull-down 130/65, pull-up 115/65 and access 100/75 nm, about 1 µm² at 90 nm and 0.25 µm² at 45 nm.","pg":108,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-10","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p109-1.png","cap":"At low VDD the 6T cell degrades, so read and write assists or a different bitcell are needed.","pg":109,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-10","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p110-1.png","cap":"Leakage is cut by lowering cell VDD, raising cell GND, sleep transistors, lowering Vgs or Vds of the access paths, or reverse body bias.","pg":110,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-10","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p111-1.png","cap":"The 8T cell adds a separate read port (RWL, RBL) so reads never disturb the storage nodes, at about 30% area cost.","pg":111,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-10","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p111-2.png","cap":"The 10T subthreshold cell adds a read buffer that cuts bitline leakage and works down to 380 mV at 65 nm.","pg":111,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-10","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p112-1.png","cap":"Each column needs bitline conditioning, sense amplifiers and column multiplexing.","pg":112,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-10","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p112-2.png","cap":"PMOS devices precharge both bitlines high before a read, and an equalizer shorts them so the sense amp starts from zero differential.","pg":112,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-10","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p112-3.png","cap":"With 128 cells on a bitline, a big C discharged by a small cell current is slow, so sense amps fire on a small swing.","pg":112,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-10","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p112-4.png","cap":"A clocked latch-type sense amp fires after enough swing develops, with isolation devices cutting off the bitline capacitance.","pg":112,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-10","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p113-1.png","cap":"A 2k-word × 16-bit memory folded into 256 rows × 128 columns needs sixteen 8:1 column muxes.","pg":113,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-10","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p113-2.png","cap":"Hierarchical blocks shorten wires and let the block address activate only one block.","pg":113,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-10","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p114-1.png","cap":"Column muxes can be NMOS pass-transistor trees with precharged outputs, decoded directly by A0 to A2.","pg":114,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-11","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p117-1.png","cap":"A synchronous path is launch register R1, combinational logic, and capture register R2, all on one clock.","pg":117,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-11","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p117-2.png","cap":"Setup and hold bracket a window around the clock edge where D must be stable; tc-q is measured from the edge to Q.","pg":117,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-11","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p118-1.png","cap":"With ideal clocks the cycle must cover tc-q + logic + tsu, and the fastest path must outlast thold.","pg":118,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-11","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p119-1.png","cap":"Skew is spatial variation of the same edge across the chip; jitter is temporal variation of successive edges at one point.","pg":119,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-11","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p119-2.png","cap":"Both skew and jitter eat into the usable cycle time.","pg":119,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-11","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p119-3.png","cap":"Clock uncertainty comes from the generator, devices, supply, interconnect, temperature, load, and coupling.","pg":119,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-11","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p119-4.png","cap":"Measured skew across an IBM processor's paths spans tens of picoseconds around the nominal arrival.","pg":119,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-11","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p120-1.png","cap":"Clock routed with the data gives positive skew; clock routed against the data gives negative skew.","pg":120,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-11","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p121-1.png","cap":"Positive skew gives the logic extra time but makes hold harder, and slowing the clock cannot fix a hold violation.","pg":121,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-11","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p121-2.png","cap":"Negative skew shortens the effective cycle but relaxes hold.","pg":121,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-11","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p122-1.png","cap":"Setup with skew: T ≥ tc-q + tsu + tlogic − δ, worst when the receiving edge comes early.","pg":122,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-11","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p122-2.png","cap":"Hold with skew: tc-q,cd + tlogic,cd > thold + δ, worst when the receiving edge comes late.","pg":122,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-11","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p125-1.png","cap":"A good flop needs small tc-q, tsu and thold, low clock load and power, drive strength, scan, and robustness.","pg":125,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-11","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p125-2.png","cap":"Buffer the input, never float storage nodes, give them modest capacitance, and keep their wires short.","pg":125,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-11","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p127-1.png","cap":"The C²MOS dynamic register uses clocked inverters for master and slave, with storage on C1 and C2.","pg":127,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-11","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p128-1.png","cap":"TSPC latches use a single clock with no inverted phase: the negative latch holds at clk=1, the positive latch is transparent at clk=1.","pg":128,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-11","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p128-2.png","cap":"An edge-triggered cell can be two latches in master-slave form or one latch opened by a short pulse.","pg":128,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-11","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p129-1.png","cap":"A glitch generator ANDs CLK with a delayed inverted CLK to make a short CLKG pulse that opens a single C²MOS-style latch.","pg":129,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-11","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p129-2.png","cap":"The HLFF used in AMD K6 and K7 merges a pulse window and a latch in one cell.","pg":129,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-11","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p130-1.png","cap":"Clocks are not ideal, skew should stay under about 10% of the period, and sequentials consume much of the timing and power budget.","pg":130,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-12","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p132-1.png","cap":"Adding k words with k−1 ordinary adders is large and slow because every adder propagates a carry.","pg":132,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-12","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p132-2.png","cap":"A carry-save adder is N independent full adders that reduce three words to a sum word and a carry word.","pg":132,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-12","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p133-1.png","cap":"Use k−2 CSA stages to keep the sum in redundant carry-save form, then one carry-propagate adder at the end.","pg":133,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-12","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p134-1.png","cap":"An M×N multiply forms N shifted M-bit partial products and sums them into an M+N-bit product.","pg":134,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-12","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p134-2.png","cap":"The product is a double sum of AND terms xi·yj weighted by 2^(i+j).","pg":134,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-12","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p134-3.png","cap":"A dot diagram shows every partial-product bit as a dot, making the parallelogram shape of the sum visible.","pg":134,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-12","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p134-4.png","cap":"An array multiplier sums partial products with rows of carry-save adders and finishes with a carry-propagate adder.","pg":134,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-12","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p135-1.png","cap":"The array can be squashed into a rectangular floorplan for regular layout.","pg":135,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-12","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p135-2.png","cap":"Radix-2^r encoding looks at r multiplier bits at a time, cutting the number of partial products to N/r.","pg":135,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-12","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p135-3.png","cap":"Booth encoding replaces 3Y with −Y plus 4Y in the next partial product, so only 0, ±Y and ±2Y are needed.","pg":135,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-12","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p135-4.png","cap":"A Booth encoder produces SINGLE, DOUBLE and NEG per partial product, and a selector per bit chooses and inverts.","pg":135,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-12","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p137-1.png","cap":"Negative partial products need sign extension, which adds many bits and puts a heavy load on each sign bit.","pg":137,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-12","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p137-2.png","cap":"Because the sign field is all 0s or all 1s, it can be replaced by all 1s plus a correction in one column.","pg":137,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-12","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p137-3.png","cap":"All the constant 1s can be summed at design time, leaving only a few sign-related bits per partial product.","pg":137,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-12","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p138-1.png","cap":"A Wallace tree reduces partial products with full and half adders in parallel layers, cutting the height by 3:2 per level.","pg":138,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-12","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p138-2.png","cap":"In the 4×4 Wallace tree the critical path is only two adder stages before the final adder.","pg":138,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-12","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p138-3.png","cap":"A 4:2 compressor built from two full adders reduces four bits to two with a lateral carry that does not ripple.","pg":138,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-12","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p138-4.png","cap":"The final adder sees partial-product bits arriving at different times, with the middle columns latest.","pg":138,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-13","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p140-1.png","cap":"Pitch = w + s and aspect ratio t/w ≈ 2 in modern processes, packing many tall, skinny wires.","pg":140,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-13","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p140-2.png","cap":"Modern stacks have 6-10+ layers: thin dense M1 for cells, wider mid layers, thickest top layers for VDD, GND and clock.","pg":140,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-13","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p141-1.png","cap":"A distributed wire is approximated by N lumped segments; the π-model is preferred and the L-model overestimates delay.","pg":141,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-13","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p141-2.png","cap":"R = ρl/(tw) = R□·(l/w): count squares, since a square of any size has the same resistance.","pg":141,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-13","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p141-3.png","cap":"Aluminum until the 180 nm generation, copper since; Cu 1.7 vs Al 2.8 µΩ·cm bulk resistivity.","pg":141,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-13","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p141-4.png","cap":"Contacts and vias are 2-20 Ω each, so use many in parallel for low resistance.","pg":141,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-13","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p142-1.png","cap":"Ctotal = Ctop + Cbot + 2·Cadj: plates above and below plus both neighbors.","pg":142,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-13","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p142-2.png","cap":"C grows with overlap area (w, t) and shrinks with distance (s, h); k = 3.9 for SiO2, about 3 or less for low-k.","pg":142,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-13","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p143-1.png","cap":"Diffusion is 1-2 fF/µm and resistive and poly is highly resistive: never use them as wires.","pg":143,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-13","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p143-2.png","cap":"Capacitive coupling to a switching neighbor injects noise into quiet wires and changes delay of switching ones.","pg":143,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-13","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p144-1.png","cap":"Effective coupling capacitance depends on the neighbor: Miller factor 1 if quiet, 0 if switching the same way, 2 if switching opposite.","pg":144,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-13","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p144-2.png","cap":"A floating victim sees ΔVvictim = Cadj/(Cgnd−v + Cadj)·ΔVaggressor - a capacitive divider.","pg":144,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-13","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p145-1.png","cap":"A driven victim fights back: noise is divided by (1 + k), where k is the ratio of aggressor to victim time constants.","pg":145,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-13","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p145-2.png","cap":"For Cadj = Cvictim the floating victim reaches 50%, while victims with half-, equal- and double-size drivers peak at 16%, 8% and 4%.","pg":145,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-13","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p145-3.png","cap":"Noise below the noise margin is harmless to static CMOS, but glitches cost delay and power, and dynamic logic and memories can fail outright.","pg":145,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-13","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p145-4.png","cap":"Wire engineering trades width, spacing, layer and shielding: wider pitch lowers both RC/2 delay and the coupling fraction.","pg":145,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-13","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p146-1.png","cap":"RC delay grows as l², so break long wires into N segments, each driven by an inverter or buffer.","pg":146,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-13","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p146-2.png","cap":"Each segment is modeled as an inverter of width W (R/W, CW·pinv) driving a π-model wire of length l/N and the next repeater's gate CW.","pg":146,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-13","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p147-1.png","cap":"Minimizing Elmore delay gives segment length l/N = √(2RC′/(RwCw)) and width W = √(RCw/(RwC′)), with C′ = C(1 + pinv).","pg":147,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"427-13","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs427-lecture-packet-p147-2.png","cap":"Delay-optimal repeaters cost 1.87·Cw·VDD² per length, an 87% premium; downsizing for minimum EDP cuts the premium to 30% for 14% more delay.","pg":147,"w":692,"h":517},{"lec":"627-1","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p6-1.jpeg","cap":"A quiet victim coupled to a switching aggressor sees four glitch types, low/high undershoot and low/high overshoot, depending on its own state and the aggressor direction.","pg":6,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-1","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p6-2.jpeg","cap":"The distributed RC aggressor/victim pair reduces to a Thevenin aggressor source, the victim holding resistance Rh, the victim ground cap Cg and the coupling cap Cc.","pg":6,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-1","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p7-1.jpeg","cap":"Ignoring the holder during a fast edge gives the charge-divider bound ΔVN = ΔVa·Cc/(Cg+Cc).","pg":7,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-1","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p7-2.jpeg","cap":"A stronger victim driver, weaker aggressor, larger Cg or smaller Cc all lower the peak noise, and each one changes the waveform's rise or fall differently.","pg":7,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-1","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p8-1.jpeg","cap":"When a dynamic node shares charge with an internal stack node, Vo = Vdd·Co/(Co+Ci), unless the internal node clamps at Vdd − Vt first.","pg":8,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-1","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p8-2.jpeg","cap":"Ground and supply networks carry current, so the driver's and receiver's local rails differ, and a receiver can see a \"0\" that turns its NMOS on.","pg":8,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-1","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p9-1.jpeg","cap":"When undershoot noise drives a source below ground, the n+/p-sub diode forward-biases and injects electrons, which diffuse to the nearest high-voltage n+ node, such as a dynamic node at Vdd.","pg":9,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-1","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p9-2.jpeg","cap":"A Vdd-tied n+ guard ring around sensitive NMOS collects the stray electrons, and it also helps with latch-up.","pg":9,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-1","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p9-3.jpeg","cap":"Alpha particles and neutrons leave an ionization track, and the charge collected from that track is a localized, angle-dependent, non-deterministic event.","pg":9,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-1","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p9-4.jpeg","cap":"A node flips when the collected charge exceeds Qcrit ≈ Cnode·VDD, and the failure rate is the tail of the strike-charge distribution beyond Qcrit.","pg":9,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-1","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p10-1.jpeg","cap":"FIT counts failures per 10⁹ device-hours, so 1000 FIT is about 1 failure per 100 years per chip but about 1 per month across 1200 machines.","pg":10,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-1","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p10-2.jpeg","cap":"In FinFET nodes per-bit SRAM SER drops, but multi-cell upsets dominate, and unprotected flip-flops now cause about 50% of system FIT.","pg":10,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-1","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p11-1.jpeg","cap":"A normal latch keeps its state on two nodes, so after a strike makes both N0 and N1 high, the original state cannot be recovered.","pg":11,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-1","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p11-2.jpeg","cap":"Triple modular redundancy stores the bit in three flops and a majority voter outputs the value held by at least two of them.","pg":11,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-1","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p11-3.jpeg","cap":"The DICE latch stores the bit on four nodes (N0a, N1a, N0b, N1b), each driven by a PMOS gated by one neighbor and an NMOS gated by the other.","pg":11,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-1","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p11-4.jpeg","cap":"After a strike on one DICE node, two neighbors float and one is held at ½, so the two untouched nodes drive the struck node back to its original value.","pg":11,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-1","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p12-1.jpeg","cap":"Data noise flips a latch only if it lands inside the setup-hold window around the closing clock edge.","pg":12,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-1","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p12-2.jpeg","cap":"An unbuffered latch input on a long wire is vulnerable to low undershoot, because a below-ground D turns on the off transmission gate and corrupts the stored node.","pg":12,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-1","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p12-3.jpeg","cap":"A dynamic gate is exposed for the whole evaluate window and is sensitive to low overshoot on its inputs, because a glitch that discharges the precharged node cannot be undone.","pg":12,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-1","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p12-4.jpeg","cap":"Aggressor noise only matters if its propagation window lines up with the capture flop's setup-hold window, so in this example only A1 can cause a failure.","pg":12,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-1","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p13-1.jpeg","cap":"Injected noise has to be propagated until it hits a latch, and along the way each gate may attenuate it, amplify it or add new coupled noise.","pg":13,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-1","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p13-2.jpeg","cap":"Model a long inverter chain as a feedback loop, where VIL/VIH define valid inputs and NML = VIL − VOL and NMH = VOH − VIH bound the DC noise allowed per stage.","pg":13,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-1","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p14-1.jpeg","cap":"Choosing VIL = VIH at the 45° crossing gives the degenerate case NML = NMH = 0, so pushing one margin up always takes from the other.","pg":14,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-1","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p14-2.jpeg","cap":"Each stage's noise gain is ∂Vo/∂Vx = k/(1−k), so a stage stays non-amplifying only where the inverter's small-signal gain k ≤ ½.","pg":14,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-1","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p15-1.jpeg","cap":"An aggressor switching opposite to the victim during the victim's transition adds a glitch that slows the edge, which makes setup timing worse.","pg":15,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-1","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p15-2.jpeg","cap":"An aggressor switching the same way speeds the victim edge, which reduces delay and can break hold timing.","pg":15,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-1","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p15-3.jpeg","cap":"With equal slews and opposite switching, replacing Cc by 2·Cc to ground (4 + 2·2 = 8 fF on each net) reproduces the full coupled simulation.","pg":15,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-1","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p15-4.jpeg","cap":"With a 4× faster aggressor and 4 fF coupling, the matching grounded caps are 24 fF on the victim and 9 fF on the aggressor, which shows how asymmetric Miller factors become.","pg":15,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-1","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p16-1.jpeg","cap":"Spacing cuts both Cc and total cap at an area cost, and shielding eliminates Cc but increases total capacitance.","pg":16,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-1","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p16-2.jpeg","cap":"Upper metals are wider and more widely spaced (Cc ≈ 0.1Cg), lower metals are dense (Cc ≈ 2Cg), and bus encoding reduces worst-case transitions.","pg":16,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-2","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p19-1.jpeg","cap":"Deciding which of two nearly simultaneous events came first takes longer the closer they are, and fails when it takes longer than the time allotted.","pg":19,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-2","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p19-2.jpeg","cap":"A latch has two stable states and one metastable balance point where A = B = Vm.","pg":19,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-2","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p20-1.jpeg","cap":"The initial imbalance ΔV0 is proportional to the input/clock offset Δt, scaled by the aperture window ta.","pg":20,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-2","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p20-2.jpeg","cap":"Once the inputs settle, the latch is two cross-coupled inverters and ΔV grows exponentially with time constant τr = Cd/gm.","pg":20,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-2","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p21-1.jpeg","cap":"Setting ΔV(td) = 1 V gives td = τr·ln(ta/Δt), so td goes to infinity as Δt goes to 0.","pg":21,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-2","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p21-2.jpeg","cap":"For a given allotted td, the synchronizer fails only if the two edges fall within ta·e^(−td/τr) of each other.","pg":21,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-2","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p21-3.jpeg","cap":"Failure frequency is ta·fA·fB·e^(−td/τr) and MTBF is its reciprocal.","pg":21,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-2","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p21-4.jpeg","cap":"With ta = 100 ps, τr = 200 ps and td = 10 ns, MTBF comes out near 10^8 years.","pg":21,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-2","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p22-1.jpeg","cap":"Moving logic out of the crossing cycle raises td from TA − tlogic − tsu to TA − tsu.","pg":22,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-2","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p22-2.jpeg","cap":"A third stage multiplies two independent failure probabilities, so the exponent effectively doubles.","pg":22,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-2","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p23-1.jpeg","cap":"Never synchronize the same asynchronous signal in more than one place, because the copies can resolve differently.","pg":23,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-2","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p23-2.jpeg","cap":"Synchronizing each bit of a bus separately lets skew put different bits into different cycles.","pg":23,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-2","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p23-3.jpeg","cap":"Handshaking synchronizes one REQ/ACK pair instead of the data bus, so the multi-bit data is stable when it is read.","pg":23,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-2","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p23-4.jpeg","cap":"Smaller cap, waiting longer, lower frequency, faster gates, completion logic and more stages all reduce failure frequency.","pg":23,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-2","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p24-1.jpeg","cap":"Skewed inverters detect a mid-rail value and hold en low, but en can itself go metastable.","pg":24,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-2","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p24-2.jpeg","cap":"A Jamb latch resolves faster because its feedback reduces to a lightly loaded cross-coupled inverter pair.","pg":24,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-2","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p25-1.jpeg","cap":"The dynamic synchronizer uses precharged dynamic buffers whose skewed inverters turn an intermediate voltage into a pulse that resolves cleanly.","pg":25,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-2","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p25-2.jpeg","cap":"Adding capacitance stretches τ until the metastability window is measurable, then the line is extrapolated back to the self-load.","pg":25,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-3","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p27-1.jpeg","cap":"Short-circuit current flows only while both devices conduct, so a slow input driving a fast output is the bad case.","pg":27,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-3","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p27-2.jpeg","cap":"On a rising output the supply delivers C·VDD², half burned in the PMOS and half stored on the load.","pg":27,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-3","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p28-1.jpeg","cap":"On the falling edge the supply delivers nothing, and the NMOS dissipates the ½CV² stored on the load.","pg":28,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-3","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p28-2.jpeg","cap":"Switching power is α·C·VDD²·f, with α = 1 for a clock and ½ for a signal that switches once per cycle.","pg":28,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-3","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p29-1.jpeg","cap":"Gating 90% of the flip-flops in an MPEG4 decoder cut power from 30.6 mW to 8.5 mW, about 70%, using a latch-based glitch-free gate.","pg":29,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-3","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p30-1.jpeg","cap":"Two parallel copies at fref/2 each get twice the time, so VDD can drop and power scales by about ε², because the (2+ov)/2 factor is almost 1.","pg":30,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-3","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p30-2.jpeg","cap":"Pipelining halves the logic depth per stage, so VDD can drop at the same clock: with ov = 10% and ε = 0.66, power is 0.48·Pref.","pg":30,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-3","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p31-1.jpeg","cap":"In clustered voltage scaling only non-critical gates move to VDDL, and paths may go from high to low voltage only, with level-shifting flops at the boundary.","pg":31,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-3","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p31-2.jpeg","cap":"Single-rail cells at VDDH and VDDL need N-well isolation, so they go in dedicated rows or dedicated regions.","pg":31,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-3","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p31-3.jpeg","cap":"With a shared N-well tied to VDDH, VDDL cells mix freely in a row, but their PMOS sees reverse body bias VSB = VDDL − VDDH.","pg":31,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-3","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p31-4.jpeg","cap":"A VDDL input cannot turn off a VDDH PMOS, so the level-converting flip-flop restores full VDDH swing inside the flop.","pg":31,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-3","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p32-1.jpeg","cap":"For a task needing only part of peak throughput, clock or power gating saves energy linearly with duty cycle, while just-in-time DVS saves roughly with its cube.","pg":32,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-3","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p33-1.jpeg","cap":"Lowering VDD cuts active energy quadratically, but below threshold the exponential delay makes leakage energy explode, so total energy has a minimum at Vmin.","pg":33,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-3","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p33-2.jpeg","cap":"At low voltage, delay sensitivity to VDD, Vth and temperature grows exponentially, as the 65 nm inverter-chain variability plot shows.","pg":33,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-3","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p33-3.jpeg","cap":"Near-threshold computing backs off from Vmin: about 2× the minimum energy buys about 100× less delay than subthreshold, while staying about 10× below superthreshold energy.","pg":33,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-3","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p34-1.jpeg","cap":"In a DCVS converter the VDDL-driven NMOS must overpower the cross-coupled VDDH PMOS (IN-ON > IP-ON) while the PMOS must beat NMOS leakage (IP-ON > IN-OFF), and both margins collapse as VDDL drops.","pg":34,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-3","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p34-2.jpeg","cap":"The pass-gate converter reduces contention because the fighting PMOS M3 loses VGS as IN rises and M1's VDS collapses once IN is high.","pg":34,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-3","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p35-1.jpeg","cap":"Splitting the keeper into series devices (STR1) or driving it from an inverter (STR2) weakens contention without the extra node-N load of a long-channel keeper.","pg":35,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-3","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p35-2.jpeg","cap":"For VDDL = 0.3 V to VDDH = 2.5 V, zero-Vth thick-oxide devices shield the thin-oxide inputs, but plain DCVS yields only 64.72% in 100,000 Monte Carlo runs.","pg":35,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-3","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p36-1.jpeg","cap":"Interrupting the DCVS PMOS path weakens it, but the fight is still 0.3 V of NMOS VGS against 2.2 V of PMOS VSG, and yield only reaches 73.77%.","pg":36,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-3","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p36-2.jpeg","cap":"The split-control converter adds diodes so the weak NMOS pulls down a node behind off diodes and turns on the opposite PMOS, raising yield to 98.93%.","pg":36,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-3","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p36-3.jpeg","cap":"In the SLC the output buffer runs super-threshold in one state and fully off in the other, but the scheme requires VDDH ≫ 2VD.","pg":36,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-4","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p38-1.jpeg","cap":"The supply network must look like a low impedance at every frequency of interest, and Z ≤ r·VDD²/P gives about 1 mΩ for a 100 W, 1 V chip with 10% ripple.","pg":38,"w":820,"h":454},{"lec":"627-4","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p38-2.jpeg","cap":"Electrically, the regulator feeds the chip through board, package and bump/bond R and L, with bulk, ceramic, package and on-chip capacitors along the way, each with its own parasitic R and L.","pg":38,"w":820,"h":454},{"lec":"627-4","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p39-1.jpeg","cap":"IR drop scales with current (15× from low-end to high-performance), but L·di/dt scales with both current and edge rate (225×).","pg":39,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-4","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p39-2.jpeg","cap":"IR and L·di/dt drops peak at different times and places, so they do not simply add, and fast mode changes (20 A to 100 A) excite the inductive drop.","pg":39,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-4","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p39-3.jpeg","cap":"A sustained supply drop slows every gate, and AC supply noise acts like a noise pulse that can ring, overshoot and undershoot.","pg":39,"w":820,"h":454},{"lec":"627-4","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p39-4.jpeg","cap":"Inductive overshoot adds oxide stress (TDDB) and limits VDD, while high current densities drive electromigration that breaks the metal.","pg":39,"w":820,"h":454},{"lec":"627-4","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p40-1.jpeg","cap":"A real capacitor has series L, so its impedance falls only until the LC resonance and rises again above it, which makes a capacitor useless at high frequency.","pg":40,"w":820,"h":454},{"lec":"627-4","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p40-2.jpeg","cap":"Large board caps with ~100 pH, package caps with ~10 pH and on-chip decap behind ~1 pH C4 inductance each cover their own band, and together they flatten the impedance.","pg":40,"w":820,"h":454},{"lec":"627-4","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p41-1.jpeg","cap":"Wire bonds are cheap but carry ~5 nH and ~200 pads, while flip-chip area arrays bring ~0.1 nH per bump and ~1000 pads spread over the die.","pg":41,"w":820,"h":454},{"lec":"627-4","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p41-2.jpeg","cap":"Package IR drop is non-uniform across the C4 bumps and hard to predict, as the bump-current map shows.","pg":41,"w":820,"h":454},{"lec":"627-4","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p42-1.jpeg","cap":"A full grid has lots of metal, lower resistance and current density, lines up with C4 bumps and is highly redundant, at the cost of metal layers and routing resources.","pg":42,"w":820,"h":454},{"lec":"627-4","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p42-2.jpeg","cap":"Power enters through C4 balls into fully dense top metal layers, then strapped stripes and via stacks carry it down to the M2 standard-cell rails.","pg":42,"w":820,"h":454},{"lec":"627-4","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p43-1.jpeg","cap":"Every idle gate places its device and wire capacitances between VDD and GND, and averaged over both states Ceff ≈ ½(Cgs + Cdb + Cw) + Cgd.","pg":43,"w":820,"h":454},{"lec":"627-4","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p43-2.jpeg","cap":"Back of the envelope: implicit decap is about half of the non-switching capacitance, which with s ≈ 0.1 is much larger than the switching capacitance.","pg":43,"w":820,"h":454},{"lec":"627-4","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p44-1.jpeg","cap":"A single thin-oxide MOS decap can short and leaks, while two stacked caps short only on a double failure and see half the voltage, at the cost of less total capacitance.","pg":44,"w":820,"h":454},{"lec":"627-4","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p44-2.jpeg","cap":"Gating the decap through a self-biased series PMOS (or a fuse) turns it off if its oxide fails, and the self-gated version needs Isub,p > Igate,n to start up.","pg":44,"w":820,"h":454},{"lec":"627-4","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p44-3.jpeg","cap":"An active decap switches two C/2 capacitors from parallel to series during a droop, delivering ΔQ = C(Vdd + ΔV)/2 instead of a passive cap's ΔV·C.","pg":44,"w":820,"h":454},{"lec":"627-4","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p45-1.jpeg","cap":"A lumped L-R feeding on-chip C gives an IR step plus a damped ring at ωr = 1/√(LC), with the peak drop a quarter period after the current step.","pg":45,"w":820,"h":454},{"lec":"627-4","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p45-2.jpeg","cap":"IR drop needs more metal, L·di/dt needs decap (which helps only as √(1/C)) or a slow ramp, and resonance needs lower Q.","pg":45,"w":820,"h":454},{"lec":"627-4","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p46-1.jpeg","cap":"Every fix trades something: more metal lowers IR but raises Q, less L raises ωr and lowers Q, more C helps only as √C at an area cost, and slow turn-on lowers ΔI.","pg":46,"w":820,"h":454},{"lec":"627-4","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p46-2.jpeg","cap":"The Pentium 4 supply impedance peaks near 100 MHz from package L, and a current step produces first, second and third droops from on-chip, package and board capacitance.","pg":46,"w":820,"h":454},{"lec":"627-4","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p47-1.jpeg","cap":"Measured on the package power plane of a 400 MHz design, switching from NOP to a high-power instruction produces a 160 mV maximum undershoot.","pg":47,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-5","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p49-1.jpeg","cap":"A thin Si film (PD ~150 nm, FD <50 nm) sits on a ~400 nm buried oxide over a ~700 um handle wafer.","pg":49,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-5","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p49-2.jpeg","cap":"SOI removes S/D junction cap and junction leakage, is rad-hard and latch-up free, but PD-SOI has a floating body.","pg":49,"w":820,"h":451},{"lec":"627-5","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p50-1.jpeg","cap":"Smart Cut splits a hydrogen-implanted wafer at the implant plane so the donor wafer can be reused, which made SOI cheap enough for mainstream use.","pg":50,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-5","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p50-2.jpeg","cap":"Capacitive coupling through Cgb, Csb and Cdb changes the floating body voltage instantly on every transition.","pg":50,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-5","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p50-3.jpeg","cap":"DC currents (diode, leakage, tunneling, impact ionization) set the body voltage slowly, from ns to ms time constants.","pg":50,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-5","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p51-1.jpeg","cap":"Impact ionization near the drain injects holes into an NMOS body during transitions, worst at Vgs ~ Vdd/2 and Vds = Vdd.","pg":51,"w":820,"h":451},{"lec":"627-5","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p51-2.jpeg","cap":"At DC the body settles where charge in (DB leakage, impact ionization) balances charge out (BS forward diode).","pg":51,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-5","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p52-1.jpeg","cap":"First switching follows a long idle period; second switching follows a recent transition, and their delays differ.","pg":52,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-5","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p52-2.jpeg","cap":"First switch of an NMOS: body starts at 0.35 V, Cgb couples it up, Cdb couples it down 0.45 V, ending at -0.1 V.","pg":52,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-5","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p52-3.jpeg","cap":"Second switch: the body starts at 0 V and is coupled up 0.45 V by Cgb and Cdb, so the second transition is faster than the first.","pg":52,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-5","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p53-1.jpeg","cap":"With source and drain both high the body floats up; pulling the source low turns on the lateral n-p-n bipolar regardless of the gate.","pg":53,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-5","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p54-1.jpeg","cap":"FD-SOI (<50 nm film) fully depletes, removing the bipolar and most hysteresis and giving ~70 mV/dec swing.","pg":54,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-5","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p54-2.jpeg","cap":"UTBB-SOI (7 nm body, 25 nm BOX, ST 28/22 nm) adds a strong back-bias knob and an undoped channel with low RDF.","pg":54,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-5","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p54-3.jpeg","cap":"Tying the body to ground makes a 4-terminal device: slower than bulk, but no delay variation and no bipolar.","pg":54,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-5","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p54-4.jpeg","cap":"Delay vs. Leff: floating-body SOI is fastest, bulk is next, and body-tied SOI is slowest.","pg":54,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-5","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p55-1.jpeg","cap":"Shorting body to gate gives low Vth when on and high Vth when off, but only works for Vdd < 0.6 V.","pg":55,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-6","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p57-1.jpeg","cap":"A CMOS inverter driven by a step burns ½CV² in the switch on each transition; the capacitor itself stores energy without loss.","pg":57,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-6","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p57-2.jpeg","cap":"Ramping the input slowly over T cuts the loss to (RC/T)·CV², which goes to zero as T grows.","pg":57,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-6","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p57-3.jpeg","cap":"Ramping in n equal steps cuts the loss to ½CV²/n, which also goes to zero for large n.","pg":57,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-6","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p57-4.jpeg","cap":"With an oscillating source, close the switch only when the voltage across it is zero, then ride the waveform up or down.","pg":57,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-6","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p58-1.jpeg","cap":"An LC tank with a switch moves energy between inductor and capacitor every half cycle, which is lossless if Rswitch = 0.","pg":58,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-6","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p58-2.jpeg","cap":"Switch, wire and inductor resistance damp the oscillation, and the resonant frequency is fixed by L and C.","pg":58,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-6","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p59-1.jpeg","cap":"Practical drivers kick the tank at its trough, at both peak and trough, or through a square-wave source into a large tank cap.","pg":59,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-6","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p59-2.jpeg","cap":"Single-rail adiabatic OR: pass transistors gate the power clock to the output, which tracks it minus a Vt drop.","pg":59,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-6","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p59-3.jpeg","cap":"Adiabatic logic needs no clock gating and costs little energy, but needs a high-Q resonant clock and correct timing, and spends one clock phase per gate.","pg":59,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-6","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p60-1.jpeg","cap":"A dual-rail inverter with cross-coupled PMOS gives full-rail outputs and a data-independent load, at the cost of 4 transistors and strict timing.","pg":60,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-6","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p60-2.jpeg","cap":"Cascaded stages use power clocks 90 degrees apart, cycling input switch, evaluate, hold and precharge.","pg":60,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-6","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p60-3.jpeg","cap":"Dual-rail adiabatic needs four 90-degree power clocks, evaluates one gate per phase, and cannot connect arbitrary outputs to inputs.","pg":60,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-6","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p61-1.jpeg","cap":"PTERF is a dual-rail adiabatic inverter (power-clocked PMOS pair) feeding a NOR SR latch, bridging static logic to static logic.","pg":61,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-6","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p61-2.jpeg","cap":"The output node discharges adiabatically to about Vthp and the remainder is lost non-adiabatically, hence partially adiabatic.","pg":61,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-6","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p61-3.jpeg","cap":"AMD Piledriver (2012) used a resonant clock mesh, saving 24% of clock power and 10% of total.","pg":61,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-6","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p61-4.jpeg","cap":"SRAM charge recycling shorts bitlines at staggered voltages so charge is shared, claiming up to ~90% write power saving.","pg":61,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-7","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p63-1.jpeg","cap":"At 0.18 µm, ~1000 dies spanned 30% in frequency and 20X in leakage, and the fastest dies were also the leakiest.","pg":63,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-7","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p63-2.jpeg","cap":"Performance variability (σ/µ) has grown node over node, with a dip at high-k metal gate and new rises with FinFET and EUV.","pg":63,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-7","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p64-1.jpeg","cap":"Uncertainty enters at design (modeling and analysis), manufacturing (process), and application (context: V, T, workload, aging), and each stage is modeled differently.","pg":64,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-7","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p64-2.jpeg","cap":"A 2D rule-table extractor underestimated coupling capacitance by about 4X and total capacitance by about 2X compared with a 3D field solver.","pg":64,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-7","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p65-1.jpeg","cap":"In planar devices the 3σ/µ budget was about 3% for Tox, 5-7% for L and 5% for RDF.","pg":65,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-7","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p65-2.jpeg","cap":"In FinFETs RDF drops to 2% and Tox to 1.5%, but L grows to 8-12% and fin width (10-15%) and metal gate granularity (5-8%) appear.","pg":65,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-7","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p65-3.jpeg","cap":"Soft copper dishes in wide lines and erodes in dense arrays, so metal thickness, and therefore R and C, depends on layout density.","pg":65,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-7","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p65-4.jpeg","cap":"Floating fill adds two series coupling caps with switching dependence, while grounded fill adds larger ground caps without it, and either must be modeled.","pg":65,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-7","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p66-1.jpeg","cap":"Minimum feature size is k1·λ/NA, and since 193 nm light printed features far below its wavelength for two decades, everything rides on k1 (RET) and NA.","pg":66,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-7","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p66-2.jpeg","cap":"Without OPC the printed gate length varies by about 20%, and OPC pre-distorts the mask with hammerheads and serifs to compensate.","pg":66,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-7","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p67-1.jpeg","cap":"OPC adds serifs and sub-resolution scattering bars so the printed gate matches the drawn shape.","pg":67,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-7","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p67-2.jpeg","cap":"A 180° phase shifter makes adjacent openings interfere destructively so two close features print separately instead of merging.","pg":67,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-7","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p67-3.jpeg","cap":"Pitch-split DPL prints shapes in two exposures and needs tight overlay, while SADP uses spacers to double pitch and is immune to overlay but forces one CD.","pg":67,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-7","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p67-4.jpeg","cap":"Features closer than the single-exposure spacing must go on different masks, some conflicts are fixed by stitches, but odd-cycle native conflicts cannot be fixed.","pg":67,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-7","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p68-1.jpeg","cap":"Two masks give two gate-length populations, which breaks the assumption that neighbors are correlated and increases mismatch between adjacent devices.","pg":68,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-7","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p68-2.jpeg","cap":"If the mask1 access transistor is stronger than the mask2 pull-up and pull-down on the same node, write gets easier but read stability gets worse.","pg":68,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-7","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p69-1.jpeg","cap":"Each node added a resolution trick: OPC at 180 nm, phase shift and scattering bars at 90 nm, immersion at 45 nm, double patterning at 32/22 nm, quad patterning at 12/10 nm, and EUV at 7/5 nm.","pg":69,"w":820,"h":456},{"lec":"627-7","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p69-2.jpeg","cap":"Fewer dopant atoms per channel means σVth/µVth grows as Na^−0.6, so lowering doping makes relative variation worse unless HKMG helps.","pg":69,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-7","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p70-1.jpeg","cap":"Line edge roughness makes L vary along the gate width, so the device leaks like its shortest slice and Ion/Ioff degrades.","pg":70,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-7","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p70-2.jpeg","cap":"Channel stress depends on source/drain length and contact placement, so two devices with the same W and L can differ by up to ~15% (PMOS).","pg":70,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-7","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p71-1.jpeg","cap":"Local anneal temperature varies by more than 10 °C on 45 nm chips with layout pattern, and hotter anneal lowers Vth and Rext, raising both Ion and Ioff.","pg":71,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-7","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p71-2.jpeg","cap":"Variation mechanisms change physical parameters, which change electrical parameters, which change circuit performance, and one physical parameter hits several electrical ones.","pg":71,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-7","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p72-1.jpeg","cap":"Variation is systematic or random, random splits into die-to-die and within-die, and within-die splits into spatially correlated and uncorrelated, each with its own design fix.","pg":72,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-7","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p72-2.jpeg","cap":"A 130 nm wafer CD map shows smooth spatial correlation across the wafer plus a repeating within-reticle signature.","pg":72,"w":820,"h":456},{"lec":"627-7","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p73-1.jpeg","cap":"For a 2-gate path with dnom=100, σd2d=10 and σwid=5, a full corner gives 290 and zero mismatch, while local Monte Carlo at the global corner gives 281 and a 3σ mismatch of 21.","pg":73,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-7","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p74-1.jpeg","cap":"Supply varies within the die through IR drop in the resistive grid and L·di/dt droop through package inductance.","pg":74,"w":820,"h":451},{"lec":"627-7","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p74-2.jpeg","cap":"Activity creates temperature hot spots, with up to 30.3 °C rise on a processor, which shift speed and leakage locally.","pg":74,"w":820,"h":451},{"lec":"627-7","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p75-1.jpeg","cap":"Broken Si-H bonds at the interface cause NBTI and hot-carrier degradation, broken Si-O bonds cause TDDB, and drive current drops within months.","pg":75,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-7","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p75-2.jpeg","cap":"NBTI ΔVth and ΔIDS grow with stress time, and with scaling ΔVth in mV falls while the percentage Ion loss rises.","pg":75,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-7","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p76-1.jpeg","cap":"Electromigration fails where metal flux divergence occurs: more atoms in than out piles up into hillocks and shorts, and more out than in leaves voids and opens.","pg":76,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-8","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p79-1.jpeg","cap":"DVFS adapts to workload, while AVFS adapts to process, voltage, temperature and aging, and many techniques do both at once.","pg":79,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-8","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p79-2.jpeg","cap":"Variations range from extremely slow (process, aging, months) to slow (temperature, package VDD, seconds to ms) to fast (IR drop, jitter, coupling, ns), and slower ones are easier to address.","pg":79,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-8","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p80-1.jpeg","cap":"Static margins (process, aging), dynamic margins (V, T, noise) and safety margins (model uncertainty) stack on top of the point of first failure, costing power and closure effort.","pg":80,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-8","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p80-2.jpeg","cap":"Summing 3σ delay impacts of eleven mechanisms gives 129% of delay, a very conservative and unrealistic guardband.","pg":80,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-8","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p81-1.jpeg","cap":"Variations sit on a grid of spatial reach (global vs local) and temporal rate (extremely slow, slow, fast), and each adaptive technique covers a different part of the grid.","pg":81,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-8","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p81-2.jpeg","cap":"Low-power design fights robustness because low VDD raises Vt sensitivity and power optimization creates a wall of near-critical paths.","pg":81,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-8","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p81-3.jpeg","cap":"To stay safe, always raise voltage before raising frequency and lower frequency before lowering voltage.","pg":81,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-8","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p81-4.jpeg","cap":"Intel SpeedStep on XScale (0.18 µm) ran 800 MHz at 1.65 V and 900 mW down to 200 MHz at 0.75 V and 50 mW, switching frequency by slow PLL relocking.","pg":81,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-8","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p82-1.jpeg","cap":"IBM DPM avoids slow PLL relocking by running the PLL at fixed frequency and selecting divided clocks with a glitch-free mux, with VDD scaled externally from 0.9 to 1.95 V.","pg":82,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-8","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p82-2.jpeg","cap":"Design-time DVFS tables address none of the process, environmental or aging variations, because one table is used for every die.","pg":82,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-8","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p83-1.jpeg","cap":"AVFS gives each die, and each moment of its life, its own operating point, and needs a sensor, a knob and an algorithm.","pg":83,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-8","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p83-2.jpeg","cap":"Post-silicon tester tables address only variations fixed for the chip's lifetime, both global and local process.","pg":83,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-8","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p84-1.jpeg","cap":"A critical path replica is launched every reference cycle, and if its output arrives before the reference-clock sample the counter steps VDD down, otherwise up, so VDD settles where the replica just meets fref.","pg":84,"w":820,"h":451},{"lec":"627-8","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p84-2.jpeg","cap":"A tunable ring oscillator that tracks the critical path runs in a closed loop that adjusts the supply until the desired frequency is reached, saving 11% to 78% energy.","pg":84,"w":820,"h":451},{"lec":"627-8","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p84-3.jpeg","cap":"TI SmartReflex (45 nm) combines adaptive body bias (FBB for speed, RBB for leakage), DVFS and AVS, with modes that cut power to well below baseline.","pg":84,"w":820,"h":451},{"lec":"627-8","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p85-1.jpeg","cap":"A canary only tracks the real critical path across voltage approximately, and since the real critical path depends on the instruction, multiple canaries are needed to reduce the margin.","pg":85,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-8","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p85-2.jpeg","cap":"IBM Power6 uses 24 critical-path monitors with a time-to-digital converter that reads timing slack as a thermometer code and models Fmax across voltage within 3 FO2 delays.","pg":85,"w":820,"h":451},{"lec":"627-8","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p85-3.jpeg","cap":"Because the canary and the real critical path each vary locally, keeping tracking failures rare requires a 3σ margin on both distributions.","pg":85,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-8","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p85-4.jpeg","cap":"The first supply droop after a load step is fast (under 10 cycles) and can be highly localized, so a remote canary cannot see or react to it in time.","pg":85,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-8","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p86-1.jpeg","cap":"Itanium 2 regional voltage detectors spot a ~160 mV local droop and dither the regional clock from 2.272 GHz to 2.087 GHz for a droop lasting about 18 cycles.","pg":86,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-8","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p86-2.jpeg","cap":"Canary response takes a detection delay plus a response delay, during which the core keeps slowing at the environment's rate of change, so the restore margin must cover that interval.","pg":86,"w":820,"h":456},{"lec":"627-8","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p87-1.jpeg","cap":"Canary circuits address only variations that are not local or fast: D2D process, aging, package VDD and ambient temperature.","pg":87,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-8","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p87-2.jpeg","cap":"Sampling the same data at three successively delayed clocks tells whether the path is too slow, tuned, too close, or failing, so frequency can be tuned on the real circuit.","pg":87,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-8","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p88-1.jpeg","cap":"Razor I adds a shadow latch, clocked off the falling edge, to each critical flip-flop, and on a mismatch overwrites the main flop with the shadow latch's correct value.","pg":88,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-8","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p88-2.jpeg","cap":"An error is detected when data arrives after the main flop's setup time but before the shadow latch closes, and the error must propagate through XOR and OR-tree within the detection window.","pg":88,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-8","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p89-1.jpeg","cap":"Razor tunes voltage on error rate and purposely runs below the point of first failure, trading lower processor energy against rising recovery energy to find an optimal voltage.","pg":89,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-8","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p89-2.jpeg","cap":"Measured: about 0.04% error rate and 0.2% IPC loss at the optimum, with about 5% extra energy reduction past the point of first failure.","pg":89,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-8","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p89-3.jpeg","cap":"Razor II uses a single positive-level latch plus a transition detector on its internal node, flagging any transition after a short detection-clock pulse as a timing error and sending recovery to the pipeline.","pg":89,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-8","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p90-1.jpeg","cap":"On chip TT9 at fixed 1 GHz, AVS lowered VDD to 0.97 V under a typical workload versus 1.1 V with 3% margin, saving 32% power, with a power virus pushing PoFF to 1.07 V.","pg":90,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-8","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p90-2.jpeg","cap":"Razor-based tuning addresses all variations on the grid, fast ones included, as long as they fit inside the detection window.","pg":90,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-9","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p92-1.jpeg","cap":"A MOSFET leaks through three paths, source-to-drain (subthreshold), gate (oxide tunneling) and junction (drain/source to body).","pg":92,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-9","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p92-2.jpeg","cap":"Below threshold the drain current is exponential in VGS − VTH, with a swing S = n(kT/q)ln10 of 60-100 mV/decade.","pg":92,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-9","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p93-1.jpeg","cap":"In a short-channel device the drain field lowers the source barrier, so raising VDS lowers VT and raises Ioff sharply.","pg":93,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-9","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p93-2.jpeg","cap":"With DIBL (λd) and body effect (β) the leakage becomes exponential in VDS, making leakage power a strong function of VDD.","pg":93,"w":820,"h":461},{"lec":"627-9","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p94-1.jpeg","cap":"GIDL: with the gate driven negative and the drain high, band-to-band tunneling in the gate-drain overlap creates a drain-to-bulk current that grows with VDG.","pg":94,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-9","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p94-2.jpeg","cap":"In 90 nm planar NMOS, ID vs VGS shows subthreshold rising with VDS (DIBL) and a GIDL tail that rises again for negative VGS at high VDS.","pg":94,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-9","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p94-3.jpeg","cap":"In FinFET/GAA nodes the Vth drop with temperature dominates the mobility loss, so Ion increases with temperature (temperature inversion) and both Ion and Ioff rise when hot.","pg":94,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-9","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p94-4.jpeg","cap":"Gate tunneling current density is exponential in oxide thickness and voltage, only moderately temperature dependent, and larger for NMOS than PMOS.","pg":94,"w":820,"h":461},{"lec":"627-9","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p95-1.jpeg","cap":"EOT = tg·(3.9/εg): a physically thicker high-k film gives the same capacitance as thin SiO2 with far less tunneling.","pg":95,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-9","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p95-2.jpeg","cap":"Junction leakage comes from generation, diffusion and band-to-band tunneling in reverse-biased diodes; it is strongly temperature dependent but usually the smallest term.","pg":95,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-9","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p96-1.jpeg","cap":"Raising Vth (ULVT to HVT) cuts leakage by 300-1000X, but delay grows 2.3X at 0.8 V and 13.8X at 0.5 V because delay depends on the VDD/Vth ratio.","pg":96,"w":820,"h":456},{"lec":"627-9","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p96-2.jpeg","cap":"In a 2-NMOS stack of a planar NAND, the intermediate node settles where the two leakage currents match, about 9X below a single off device.","pg":96,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-9","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p96-3.jpeg","cap":"Equating the leakage of the top and bottom off devices gives VM ≈ λd·VDD/(1+2λd); the stack leaks 0.12X (planar) or 0.33X (FinFET) of a single device.","pg":96,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-9","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p96-4.jpeg","cap":"Measured stack factors: planar 9/17/24X for 2/3/4 NMOS, but only 3/5/8X for FinFET because DIBL and body effect are small.","pg":96,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-9","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p97-1.jpeg","cap":"In a 3-NMOS stack, Isub depends mainly on how many devices are off (1 off: 7-11 pA, 2 off: 2-4 pA, 3 off: 1 pA), then on where the off devices sit.","pg":97,"w":820,"h":456},{"lec":"627-9","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p97-2.jpeg","cap":"Igate depends on where the on devices sit: an on device with its channel at 0 V leaks most (2 pA each), while on devices with source/drain near VDD barely leak or leak in reverse.","pg":97,"w":820,"h":456},{"lec":"627-9","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p99-1.jpeg","cap":"Leakage spreads widely across input states for single gates (NAND4: 101X) but much less for whole blocks (data path 5X, adder 1.2X, decoder 1.25X).","pg":99,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-9","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p99-2.jpeg","cap":"After the inputs switch to the low-leakage state, internal stack nodes take microseconds to hundreds of milliseconds to settle, and leakage stays elevated meanwhile.","pg":99,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-9","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p100-1.jpeg","cap":"Splitting one transistor into two series devices of W/2 keeps the input load but costs ~4X delay for ~20X less leakage (less in FinFET), a poor trade compared with raising Vth.","pg":100,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-9","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p100-2.jpeg","cap":"Combining dual-Vt with an optimal standby input state, so that only devices that are off in standby get high Vt, cuts leakage by ~9.7X vs all low-Vt.","pg":100,"w":820,"h":456},{"lec":"627-9","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p101-1.jpeg","cap":"Power gating can use a footer, a header, or both; an NMOS footer is more area efficient, and footer plus header blocks leakage under all input patterns.","pg":101,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-9","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p101-2.jpeg","cap":"Super-cutoff CMOS uses a normal-Vt thin-oxide switch driven to a negative VGS in standby, which makes it area efficient and fast when active.","pg":101,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-9","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p101-3.jpeg","cap":"In active mode the virtual rails carry IR/switching noise; in standby they collapse (virtual VDD falls, virtual GND rises) as the logic leaks through the off switch.","pg":101,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-9","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p101-4.jpeg","cap":"Decap on the virtual rail helps performance and gets gated with the block, but lengthens the convergence time and needs a slow header turn-on to limit in-rush current.","pg":101,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-9","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p103-1.jpeg","cap":"A state-retaining MTCMOS latch keeps a high-Vt, always-powered inverter pair in the feedback loop while the low-Vt forward path is gated, at the cost of setup time.","pg":103,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-9","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p103-2.jpeg","cap":"With only one polarity of sleep device, a sneak path through the always-on high-Vt loop and the gated low-Vt devices still leaks, so both header and footer are needed.","pg":103,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-9","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p104-1.jpeg","cap":"Sleep switches are added as \"strapper\" cells in the standard-cell rows, connecting the global VDD/GND straps to the virtual rails that feed the row.","pg":104,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-9","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p104-2.jpeg","cap":"In an ALU test chip, sleep transistors cost 6% area as PMOS headers and 3% as NMOS footers, for a ~20-100X leakage reduction.","pg":104,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-9","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p104-3.jpeg","cap":"Dynamic body biasing applies forward body bias in active mode (low Vt, fast) and reverse body bias in standby (high Vt, low leakage), and can also compensate Vt variation.","pg":104,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-9","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p104-4.jpeg","cap":"Comparison: stacking 1-2X, power gating 10-1000X, dynamic body bias 2-20X, dual-Vt 2-3X.","pg":104,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-10","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p107-1.jpeg","cap":"Inductance belongs to a closed current loop and scales with loop area, and on chip the return path is whatever conductor carries the current back: power/ground lines, substrate or other signals.","pg":107,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-10","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p107-2.jpeg","cap":"Inductance causes oscillation, over/undershoot and inductive crosstalk, and it changes delay and sharpens transitions compared with an RC model.","pg":107,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-10","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p108-1.jpeg","cap":"At low frequency R dominates and return current spreads (low R, high L); at high frequency jωL dominates and return current crowds under the signal (high R, low L).","pg":108,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-10","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p108-2.jpeg","cap":"At very high frequency the skin effect confines current to a depth δ = √(2ρ/ωµ), about 2 µm in copper at 1 GHz, so R rises as √f.","pg":108,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-10","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p109-1.jpeg","cap":"A rising signal draws charging current from VDD that flows through the signal-to-GND capacitance into the ground grid and back through the supply.","pg":109,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-10","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p109-2.jpeg","cap":"A falling signal discharges the signal-to-VDD capacitance, so that part of the return current flows in the VDD grid.","pg":109,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-10","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p109-3.jpeg","cap":"A trapezoidal signal's spectrum is flat to 1/(π·tp), falls 20 dB/dec to 1/(π·tr) and 40 dB/dec beyond, so with tr ≈ 10 ps the content reaches about 30 GHz.","pg":109,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-10","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p109-4.jpeg","cap":"Driving an 8 mm × 7 mm grid line, the far-end voltage overshoots well above its final value and the line current peaks near 16 mA and rings.","pg":109,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-10","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p110-1.jpeg","cap":"Compared with an RC model, the RLC near end shows a plateau and the far end starts later but switches faster, so inductance shifts delay and sharpens edges.","pg":110,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-10","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p110-2.jpeg","cap":"The aggressor's current loop links flux into the victim's loop through mutual inductance m, inducing Vy = m·dIx/dt, so a unipolar aggressor current gives a bipolar victim glitch.","pg":110,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-10","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p110-3.jpeg","cap":"On a 3 mm bus the RC model predicts a small victim dip, but the RLC model shows a larger glitch of about 0.18 V of the opposite polarity.","pg":110,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-10","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p111-1.jpeg","cap":"A signal over a power grid returns through the grid wires, and the loop area is set by how far away the nearest power/ground wires are.","pg":111,"w":820,"h":452},{"lec":"627-10","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p111-2.jpeg","cap":"A denser power/ground grid gives close-by return paths and smaller loops, at the cost of more metal.","pg":111,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-10","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p111-3.jpeg","cap":"Dedicated power/ground shields on both sides of a signal keep the return path as close as possible.","pg":111,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-10","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p111-4.jpeg","cap":"Interdigitation splits a wide conductor into several narrow wires with shields in between, so every piece has a close return.","pg":111,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-10","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p112-1.jpeg","cap":"Inserting power/ground shields between bus wires reduces both capacitive and inductive coupling.","pg":112,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-10","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p112-2.jpeg","cap":"A twisted-bundle layout routes nets in opposite loops, so the flux from adjacent nets cancels.","pg":112,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-10","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p112-3.jpeg","cap":"In a repeated line, a modest inductance sharpens the edge arriving at each repeater, and the faster slopes cascade down the chain to cut overall delay.","pg":112,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-10","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p112-4.jpeg","cap":"Up to about 1.4 nH, inductance improves slew about 5× and cuts total delay to about 0.87× of RC with negligible overshoot; beyond that overshoot rises rapidly and delay grows.","pg":112,"w":820,"h":453},{"lec":"627-11","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p114-1.jpeg","cap":"External memory access costs ~1000 fJ/bit, 20X an on-chip SRAM access (50 fJ/bit) and 167X a PE operation (6 fJ/bit), so data movement dominates system energy.","pg":114,"w":820,"h":582},{"lec":"627-11","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p114-2.jpeg","cap":"Architectures evolve from von Neumann (separate storage and processor) to near-memory compute to in-memory compute, trading transferred data for energy efficiency.","pg":114,"w":820,"h":582},{"lec":"627-11","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p115-1.jpeg","cap":"Activating two wordlines at once makes the BL compute AND of the two cells (BLB gives NOR); the 01/10 case sits between 00 and 11 and has the smallest voltage margin.","pg":115,"w":820,"h":582},{"lec":"627-11","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p115-2.jpeg","cap":"In an 8T bitcell with the input on RWL and the weight stored in Q, the decoupled read port discharges RBL only when both are 1: a bitwise AND, which is a 1-bit multiply.","pg":115,"w":820,"h":582},{"lec":"627-11","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p116-1.jpeg","cap":"In a 10T CAM cell each bit computes XOR(SL, D) and pulls down the precharged match line on a mismatch, so ML stays high only if the whole word matches.","pg":116,"w":820,"h":582},{"lec":"627-11","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p116-2.jpeg","cap":"A CAM works as a routing table: the search word returns the matching address, which then indexes a RAM for the output data.","pg":116,"w":820,"h":583},{"lec":"627-11","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p117-1.jpeg","cap":"In-cell computing puts a multiplier in each bitcell (high parallelism, larger cell); in-array computing shares a local compute cell among a group of foundry 6T/8T cells (better density).","pg":117,"w":820,"h":582},{"lec":"627-11","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p117-2.jpeg","cap":"A current-domain multiplier is an AND: the cell sources Ion only when input X (on WL) and weight W are both 1; resistive memories use LRS/HRS currents instead.","pg":117,"w":820,"h":582},{"lec":"627-11","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p117-3.jpeg","cap":"Cell currents add on the bitline by Kirchhoff's current law; a clamp holds the BL at VBL and a current ADC reads Isum = ΣIi.","pg":117,"w":820,"h":582},{"lec":"627-11","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p117-4.jpeg","cap":"Charge-domain multipliers deposit ΔQ = ION·tpulse (current-based) or ΔQ = C0·VWL (coupling-based) on the read bitline.","pg":117,"w":820,"h":582},{"lec":"627-11","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p118-1.jpeg","cap":"Each cell contributes ΔVi = ΔQi/CBL, and the precharged bitline accumulates the sum by charge sharing, read out by a voltage ADC.","pg":118,"w":820,"h":582},{"lec":"627-11","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p118-2.jpeg","cap":"Multibit inputs can be applied as serial bits, wordline pulse width, pulse count, an analog wordline voltage, or an analog bitline voltage.","pg":118,"w":820,"h":580},{"lec":"627-11","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p119-1.jpeg","cap":"Multibit weights are built by separate columns plus a shifted adder tree, binary transistor sizing in the cell, multi-level cells, or binary-weighted charge sharing.","pg":119,"w":820,"h":582},{"lec":"627-11","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p119-2.jpeg","cap":"Signal margin is the worst-case gap between adjacent MAC levels, about range/levels minus variation, and reliable compute needs ADC offset below half of it.","pg":119,"w":820,"h":607},{"lec":"627-11","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p120-1.jpeg","cap":"Accumulating more rows per bitline raises throughput and energy efficiency but collapses the signal margin.","pg":120,"w":820,"h":607},{"lec":"627-11","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p120-2.jpeg","cap":"Measured transfer curves (Verma) bend away from the ideal line because cells move between operating regions across the MAC range.","pg":120,"w":820,"h":607},{"lec":"627-11","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p121-1.jpeg","cap":"Digital CIM replaces analog accumulation with digital multipliers, an adder tree and an accumulator, built with pitch-matched custom layout inside the macro.","pg":121,"w":820,"h":583},{"lec":"627-11","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p121-2.jpeg","cap":"TSMC digital CIM uses a NOR gate as the 1-bit multiplier per cell (6T 1RW, ISSCC'21) or per shared group (12T 1R1W with simultaneous read/write, ISSCC'22).","pg":121,"w":820,"h":582},{"lec":"627-11","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p121-3.jpeg","cap":"A mux-based local compute cell selects among 0, W0, W1 and a stored W0+W1 using two input bits, removing the static adder from the first stage.","pg":121,"w":820,"h":582},{"lec":"627-11","f":"assets/fig/lectures/eecs627-lecture-packet-p121-4.jpeg","cap":"Dual-rail power: MAC logic on a high-VDD, low-Vt domain for speed, while SRAM and decode use a low-VDD, high-Vt domain to keep standby current low.","pg":121,"w":820,"h":582}];
